  So notifications can't be the first subsystem built here; it's the layer that sits on top of three that don't exist
  yet. That's worth settling before design questions, because the answer changes what a notifications spec can even
  assume.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether I'm designing against an app that exists somewhere I haven't seen, or designing the app's
  foundations as part of this.

  - [1mBackend exists elsewhere[0m — this repo is a stub/frontend shell and the real task + user model lives in a service I
    should integrate against. Buys: I design a real notifications system against real events. Costs: I need to see that
    API before designing anything. Rules out nothing.
  - [1mSingle-user local app[0m — tasks live in the browser (localStorage), "users" is really "me", and notifications means
    in-app alerts + maybe the browser Notification API for changes I didn't make (background sync, other tabs). Buys:
    smallest useful thing, shippable. Costs: "users they care about" mostly collapses — there's no one else to be
    notified about. Hard to change later if multi-user is the real goal.
  - [1mMulti-user app, build the foundations[0m — this is a greenfield app and notifications is the motivating feature, so we
    design tasks, identity, subscriptions, and delivery together. Buys: the feature as literally stated. Costs: this is
    4 subsystems, which means decomposition into sequenced specs, not one. Biggest commitment.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m if there's a real backend, point me at it — that's strictly the best case. Absent that, I'd take
  multi-user-and-decompose over local-single-user, because a notification system is the one feature whose whole value
  comes from [3mother people[0m changing things; building it single-user first means rebuilding it when identity arrives.
